// hosted.ts — run a class constructor INSIDE a component's setup.
//
// An ivue constructor is setup code: `onMounted`, `onBeforeUnmount` and a
// plain `watch` land in the component that constructs the instance. A bare
// `new X.Class()` in a test has no component, so the hooks warn and drop,
// and a spec that reads "mounted" state would be proving nothing. This
// harness mounts a throwaway component whose setup is the factory, so
// every hook registers against a real instance and unmount runs the
// teardown the class declares — the same lifecycle the SFC gives it.
//
// Use it for any class whose constructor touches the lifecycle. A class
// with no hooks (a pure Static class, a hosted capability constructed by
// its owner) is constructed directly.
import { mount } from '@vue/test-utils';
import { defineComponent, h } from 'vue';

export function hosted<T>(factory: () => T): { instance: T; unmount: () => void } {
  let instance!: T;
  const wrapper = mount(
    defineComponent({
      setup() {
        instance = factory();
        return () => h('div');
      }
    })
  );
  return { instance, unmount: () => wrapper.unmount() };
}

/*
=== GENERATOR ===
Goal: Render a window of a few dozen rows over a list of any length, at the exact pixel the scroll names, with sizes learned as rows pass through.
// domain-invariant: contract — the props object is the fusion of the static types and defaults; a required prop carries no default, and a deliberately default-free knob reads as its documented fallback.
// domain-invariant: prefix-sum — the top of item i is the sum of the sizes before it, measured where known and the estimate elsewhere; the cursor walks there from wherever it last stood and the answer never depends on the walk.
// domain-invariant: inverse — the item at a pixel offset is the one whose span covers it, with the fraction inside; anchoring that item at that fraction returns the same pixel.
// domain-invariant: window — the rendered slice starts at the item under the scroll top, covers the container, and carries the base pad on both ends; an unchanged window returns the same array so the v-for does not re-render.
// domain-invariant: spacers — the leading spacer is the estimated top of the first rendered item minus the render bias; the trailing spacer is the rest of the content, capped at the render cap.
// domain-invariant: clamp — a scroll position lands inside [0, extent − container]; a non-finite position is refused and the last position stands.
// domain-invariant: rebase — past the first chunk the render bias is the chunk floor below the scroll, subtracted from the spacer and the transform alike.
// domain-invariant: scrollbar — the thumb is the container's share of the extent, floored so a million rows still present a grabbable thumb; progress is the position's share of the scrollable range.
// domain-invariant: repair — when the list shrinks, measurements beyond its end are dropped and the extent is re-derived.
// domain-invariant: axis-lock — a touch that moves along the cross axis is handed to the page by flagging the event; the own axis keeps it; a finger that has not moved past the threshold is undecided.
// domain-invariant: row-text — a row's copied text comes from the selectionText prop, else the item's body, else its id.
// domain-invariant: seek — a track fraction names an ITEM (index space), so the landing survives the estimate→real refinement.
Impossible if true: A rendered scroll position beyond the extent, an item outside the list with a position, or a window whose spacers plus rows sum to anything but the extent.

=== GENERATOR-DESCRIBED ===
Every spec runs headless: the scroller is hosted in a throwaway component
(hosted.ts) so its constructor's hooks and watch land somewhere real, and
the DOM it cannot have is stubbed at its seams — a Probe subclass pins the
container size and exposes the protected seams, the way a horizontal
subclass overrides them. Lenis is never created (no mount of the SFC), so
the padding class sees zero velocity and the window carries the base pad
only. The wheel lerp, the creep integrator and the converge loop need a
browser; they are covered by the component sweep, not here.
*/

